{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-24) — narrow + de-park, grounded in git-history + OSS research\n\n### (a) Narrowed to the Python public-API surface only\nThe config-key inventory half is **already delivered**: sibling work (ticket 3199, RP-04 S7.4)\nshipped `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` + generated `docs/config-reference.md` with a **portable,\ntest-based drift gate**. That half is dropped from scope. This ticket is now solely about keeping\nthe **Python public-API surface** drift-free.\n\n### (b) Git-history check — is there real doc-drift pain, and are the hand-authored docs complete?\n- `docs/reuse-surface.md` is touched **frequently** (~20+ commits: every RP-04 slice, bridge,\n  footprint, attestation change) — i.e. it requires **recurring manual upkeep** to stay accurate.\n- `docs/api-stability.md` is comparatively stable (~6 commits), but one was an explicit\n  `docs(drift): sync version marker …; add version guard` — drift correction did happen.\n- A **partial** CI drift guard already exists: `tests/unit/test_reuse_surface_doc.py` (ticket f5df)\n  introspects a **hand-picked, hardcoded** subset of signatures (signing, prompt library,\n  runner/contracts, workflow executor) and asserts they match the doc. It was added to remediate a\n  plan-review finding that the doc's \"verified against source (no drift)\" AC **had no proving\n  mechanism**. So: the docs are actively maintained and mostly accurate, but the guard is manual,\n  narrow (a curated subset, not the whole public surface), and must be edited by hand in lockstep\n  with signature changes.\n- Net: **recurring upkeep and a demonstrated (if remediated) drift gap — but no catastrophic silent\n  drift.** Modest, real pain; not a fire.\n\n### (c) How popular, actively-maintained OSS projects solve this\nDedicated research (requests, Flask, pydantic, FastAPI, httpx, urllib3, cryptography, attrs, +\nthe griffe/mkdocstrings ecosystem):\n- **Nobody hand-writes API signatures, and nobody fully auto-generates** an un-curated dump. The\n  universal pattern is a **hybrid**: curated structure + docstring-extracted content.\n- The emerging **drift gate** is **`griffe check`** — a CLI that diffs the current code's public\n  surface against the last release tag and fails on removed symbols / renamed params / changed\n  signatures. Adopted by `pypa/setuptools-scm` (`.github/workflows/api-check.yml`), OpenFreeEnergy,\n  AWS SDKs; griffe uses it on itself. It requires **no doc-format change** and runs in ~5s.\n- `__all__`-as-source-of-truth has pitfalls (re-exports, omissions); griffe defaults to the\n  underscore convention with an extension for `__all__` re-exports.\n\n### Recommended scope (this is what the ticket becomes)\nDo **NOT** build a bespoke `gen_api_reference.py` generated Markdown reference — the evidence says\nthat's neither the mainstream pattern nor the high-ROI move. Instead:\n1. Add a **portable Python public-API surface drift gate** modeled on `griffe check`, wired as a\n   **test** (setuptools-scm wraps griffe in a small Python script) so it is operation/test-linked\n   and runs under `make test` with **no CI-provider dependency** (satisfies `project.portability`) —\n   the direct analog of the existing config-key drift gate.\n2. **Replace** the hand-maintained `test_reuse_surface_doc.py` hardcoded signature assertions with\n   the automatic surface check (or have the gate subsume it), removing the manual lockstep burden.\n3. Keep the hand-authored `api-stability.md` / `reuse-surface.md` as the curated narrative; the gate\n   guards the *surface*, the prose stays human.\n\nDe-parking (removing the `idea` tag) and retitling to this concrete, low-priority scope. If the\noperator would rather accept the status quo (manual test + narrative docs), close as\naccepted-tradeoff instead — the pain is modest."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787594156635382001,"uuid":"82a4401c-8a1d-45fa-b8b2-a874300948e5"}